## 1. Introduction
Nickel-metal hydride batteries and lithium-ion batteries (LIBs) have been extensively applied in the field of portable electronics [1–10]. However, with the sustainable development of society and economy, the energy density and rate capability of these energy storage devices should be further improved to fulfill the requirements of the large-scale vehicle power systems for electric vehicles (EVs) and hybrid electric vehicles (HEVs) [11–16]. Since the performance of LIBs is mainly determined by their anode and cathode materials, numerous endeavors have been undertaken to explore the novel electrodes to substitute the conventional ones [17,18]. In the search of the advanced anode materials, transition metal sulfides (TMSs) have demonstrated great potential as alternatives to the conventional graphite anode owing to their abundant earth resource, intriguing crystal structure and high lithium storage capability [19–24]. Nevertheless, TMS bulks suffer from the inferior electrical conductivity, unsatisfied charge adsorption ability, and large volume expansion during charge/discharge process, leading to the poor capacity, stability and rate performance [25–27]. In this regard, the improvement of the TMS electrodes in terms of the high-rate and long-term cyclability for EVs and HEVs is significant.
In order to improve the cycle stability, hybridizing TMS with conductive carbon matrix $(\mathrm{TMS}@C)$ has been demonstrated as an effective approach because the carbon matrix not only enhances the electrical conductivity of composites but also can serve as the robust matrix to buffer the volume variation and maintain the structural stability [27–29]. For instance, Xu et al. reported that the composites constructed by rooting $\Nu\Nu_{x}C o_{1-x}S e_{2}$ nanosheets on surface of carbon dodecahedra could deliver a specific capacity of $1667\mathrm{\mAh\g^{-1}}$ at $2\mathsf{A}\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ after 600 cycles [30]. Lin et al. synthesized a composite of $\mathsf{N i S}_{2}$ and $\mathsf{C o S}_{2}$ hetero-nanocrystals encapsulated in $N\mathrm{.}$ -doped carbon nanocubes, showing a capacity of $600\ \mathrm{mAh}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ after 100 cycles at $1\mathrm{~A~g^{-1}}$ [31]. Despite great advances that have been achieved, the rate capabilities of most reported ${\mathrm{TMS}}@C$ composites are still unsatisfactory and need to be further ameliorated.
Recent studies have revealed that the hetero-cation doping can powerfully enhance the lithium storage kinetics of TMS electrodes by introducing edge/surface defects and local charge polarization, which would remarkably contribute to the charge and electron transfer [32–34]. For instance, Gao et al. encapsulated iron-doped $\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}$ on N, S-doped carbon nanosheet as anode material for LIBs, which exhibits a high rate capability of $331\mathrm{\mAh\g^{-1}}$ at $1\mathrm{~A~g~}^{-1}$ [35]. Meng et al. prepared $\mathrm{Cu}_{0.33}\mathrm{Co}_{0.67}\mathrm{S}_{2}$ hexagonal sheets and found they could achieve a dramatically improved rate capability of $558~\mathrm{mAh}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at $10\mathrm{Ag}^{-1}$ by copper cation doping [36]. In addition to the cation-doping, construction of two-dimensional (2D) architecture can also greatly increase the rate capability as its high surface-to-volume ratio can readily trigger the charge-transfer reactions including surface or near surface redox reactions as compared with their three-dimensional (3D) counterparts [37–41]. Currently, there are two ways to prepare the 2D TMS-based electrodes. One is to directly synthesize 2D architecture of TMS, the other is to anchor the TMS nanoparticles on the 2D graphene [42]. However, the former one usually makes electrode suffer from collapses during the repeated charge/discharge process [43], while the latter one usually makes electrode possess a weak coupling between TMS and graphene, which may cause particle aggregation and increase the paths for ion transportation [44,45]. As such, it is highly desired to construct a robust and well-coupled 2D TMS@Cbased nanohybrid.
Based on the above discussions, herein, we have proposed a 2D MOF-driven strategy to in-situ fabricate well-coupled ${\mathrm{TMS@C}}$ hybrid composite [46–48]. Specifically, Copper ions and 1,3,5- benzenetricarboxylic acid $(\mathsf{H}_{3}\mathsf{B T C})$ can form 2D $\mathsf{C u}(\mathrm{HBTC})(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathsf{O})_{3}$ $(\mathsf{C u}(\mathsf{H B T C})–1)$ precursor and then 2D architecture consisting of cobalt-doped copper sulfides embedding within ultrathin carbon nanosheets (2D $\mathtt{C o-C u_{2}S@C)}$ can be prepared by one-pot sulfurization of 2D cobalt-doped $\mathsf{C u}(\mathsf{H B T C}){\mathsf{-1}}$ precursor $(\mathsf{C o-C u}(\mathsf{H B T C})-1).$ . The in-situ fabrication not only allows hybridization of TMS with carbon but also ensures the strong coupling effect between the components, which is beneficial to preventing the aggregation during the iterative charge/discharge process and enhancing the charge transfer [23,35]. Such a nanohybrid through a MOF-driven strategy possesses the following merits for lithium storage: (i) the coupled carbon nanosheet functions as a buffering matrix to alleviate the volume variation of electrode during charge/discharge process and facilitates the structural stability, (ii) the Co doping in $\mathtt{C u}_{2}\mathtt{S}$ lattice could effectively improve the conductivity and accelerate the electron transfer, (iii) the porous ultrathin feature of architecture ensures a rapid mass diffusion and ion transport. Therefore, when employed as anode material for LIB, 2D ${\mathsf{I C o-C u}}_{2}{\mathsf{S@C}}$ composite exhibits a superior electrochemical performance in terms of an outstanding cycle durability $(780\ \mathrm{mAh}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at $0.5\mathrm{~A~g^{-1}}$ after 300 cycles) and excellent rate capability $(209\mathrm{~mAh~g^{-1}}$ at $5\mathrm{~A~g~}^{-1}.$ in comparison with 2D $\mathsf{C u}_{2}S@C$ 3D $\mathtt{C u}_{2}S@C$ and pure $\mathtt{C u}_{2}S$ anodes.
## 2. Experimental section
## 2.1. Chemicals
The chemicals were used as received without further purification. Copper (II) nitrate trihydrate $(\mathsf{C u}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}.3\mathsf{H}_{2}0$ , Sinopharm Chemical Reagent Co., $99.5\%$ ), copper (II) chloride dehydrate $(\mathsf{C u C l}_{2}.2\mathsf{H}_{2}0$ , Sinopharm Chemical Reagent Co., $99\%$ ), cobalt (II) nitrate hexahydrate $(\mathsf{C o}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}.6\mathsf{H}_{2}0$ , Sinopharm Chemical Reagent Co., $98+\%$ ), 1,3,5-benzenetricarboxylic acid $\mathrm{\cdotH}_{3}\mathrm{BTC}$ , Aldrich, $95\%$ ), sodium sulfide $\mathrm{\langleNa}_{2}S,$ Sinopharm Chemical Reagent Co., $99\%$ ), polyvinylpyrrolidone (PVP, Aldrich, K-30), sodium $\operatorname{L}(+)$ -ascorbate (Ourchem, $99\%$ ), sodium hydroxide (Sinopharm Chemical Reagent Co., $96\%$ ), sublimed sulfur (Aldrich, $99.5\%$ , N-methyl pyrrolidone (NMP), carbon nanotube (CNT), polyvinylidene fluoride (PVDF), methanol (Aladdin, $99\%$ ) and ethanol (Aladdin, $99.99\%$ ). Deionized water was used for all experiments.
## 2.2. Preparation of HKUST-1 precursors
HKUST-1 is another MOF constructed by copper ion and $\mathsf{H}_{3}\mathsf{B T C}$ with 3D octahedron-like structure, which was reported and named by Williams et al. at Hong Kong University of Science and Technology (HKUST) in 1999 [49]. $875~\mathrm{mg}$ of $\mathsf{H}_{3}\mathsf{B T C}$ was dissolved in methanol $\mathrm{50~mL}$ . Then a $50~\mathrm{mL}$ of methanol solution dissolved with $_{1.82\mathrm{~g~}}$ of $\mathrm{Cu}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}3\mathsf{H}_{2}0$ was added to above solution followed by stirring for $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ at room temperature. The blue precipitates were collected by centrifugation and washed by methanol for several times. HKUST-1 precursors were finally obtained after drying blue precipitates in oven at $60^{\circ}C$ overnight.
## 2.3. Preparation of 2D Co-doped Cu(HBTC)-1-1 and 2D Cu(HBTC)-1 precursors
A facile bottom-up route at room temperature and ambient pressure was used to synthesize 2D $\mathsf{C u}(\mathsf{H B T C}){\mathsf{-1}}$ and 2D Co-doped $\mathsf{C u}(\mathsf{H B T C}){\mathsf{-1}}$ (denoted as 2D Co-Cu(HBTC)-1) [50]. $136.8~\mathrm{mg}$ of $\mathrm{CuCl}_{2}{\cdot}2\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ and $800~\mathrm{mg}$ of PVP were dissolved into $160~\mathrm{{mL}}$ of deionized water. Then, $10~\mathrm{mL}$ of $0.8~\mathrm{mol}~\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ NaOH solution and $10~\mathrm{mL}$ of $0.4~\mathrm{mol}~\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ sodium $\operatorname{L}(+)$ -ascorbate solution were mixed with the above solution under stirring to form an orange mixture. After continuously stirring for $5\ \mathrm{min}$ , the mixture was centrifuged and washed with ethanol for twice to obtain orange solids. The orange solids were resuspended into $40~\mathrm{mL}$ ethanol to form $\mathtt{C u}_{2}0$ -ethanol suspension. $3.2\mathrm{~g~}$ of PVP and $425~\mathrm{mg}$ of Co $(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathsf{H}_{2}0$ were dissolved in $240\mathrm{mL}$ of deionized water and then $32~\mathrm{mL}$ of ethanol solution dissolved with $1.6{\mathrm{g}}$ of $\mathsf{H}_{3}\mathsf{B T C}$ was added to the above solution under stirring. After that, the as-prepared $40~\mathrm{\mL}$ of $\mathtt{C u}_{2}0$ -ethanol suspension was poured to the above solution followed by stirring for $36\mathrm{~h~}$ . The light blue solids were collected by centrifugation and washed with deionized water for twice. After dried in oven overnight at $60~^{\circ}\mathsf C$ , 2D $\mathsf{C o-C u(H B T C)}_{-1-}$ 1 products were obtained. The formation of $\mathsf{C u}(\mathsf{H B T C})$ -1was similar to that of 2 $)\mathsf{C o-C u(H B T C)}\mathrm{-}1\mathrm{-}1$ but using additional of Co $(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}.6\mathsf{H}_{2}0$ . Note that the ratio of ethanol to water is the key for the formation of 2D morphology. To further investigate the influence of cobalt, $\mathsf{C o-C u(H B T C)}_{-1}$ with different amounts of cobalt were synthesized. In the synthesis process, different amounts of $\mathrm{Co}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathsf{H}_{2}0$ were added to form Co-doped Cu (HBTC)-1, denoted as 2D Co-Cu(HBTC)-1-n, respectively, where the amount of used $C_{0}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ increases with number n.
2.4. Preparations of $2D C o-C u_{2}S@C,$ , 2D $C u_{2}S@C$ and 3D $C u_{2}S@C$ hybrid composites
A clean porcelain boat loaded with 2D Co-Cu(HBTC)-1-1 precursors and sulfur powders with a mass ratio of 1:1 was put into a tube furnace and then heated to $600^{\circ}\mathsf C$ with a ramp rate of $3^{\circ}C$ $\mathrm{min}^{-1}$ under flowing argon. After being maintained at this temperature for $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ , the 2D ${\mathsf{C o-C u_{2}S@C}}$ hybrid composites were successfully prepared. The preparations of 2D $\mathsf{C u}_{2}\mathsf{S@C}$ and 3D $\mathsf{C u}_{2}\mathsf{S@C}$ were similar to that making for 2D Co- $\mathsf{.C u}_{2}S@C$ hybrid composites but with 2D $\mathsf{C u}(\mathsf{H B T C}){\mathsf{-1}}$ and HKUST-1 as precursors, respectively. For comparison, 2D $\mathsf{C o-C u}_{2}\mathsf{S}@\mathsf{C}\mathsf{-}2$ and 2D ${\mathsf{C o-C u}}_{2}{\mathsf{S}}@{\mathsf{C}}{\mathsf{-}}3$ with different cobalt contents were formed via sulfurization of 2D Co-Cu (HBTC)-1–2 and 2D ${\mathsf{C o-C u}}_{2}{\mathsf{S@C}}\mathrm{-}1-3$ , respectively.
## 2.5. Preparation of pure $C u_{2}S$
$32~\mathrm{mg}$ of sodium sulfide powders were dispersed in $20~\mathrm{mL}$ of deionized water. The sodium sulfide aqueous solution was then added into the aforementioned $\mathsf{C u}_{2}0$ -ethanol suspension. The mixture was aged for $25\ \mathrm{min}$ under stirring. The black products were collected and dried. A clean porcelain boat loaded with dry black products and sulfur powders with a mass ration of 1:1 was put into a tube furnace and then heated to $600^{\circ}\mathsf C$ with a ramp rate of $3^{\circ}C$ $\mathrm{min}^{-1}$ under flowing argon. After being maintained at this temperature for $^\mathrm{~2~h~}$ , the pure 3D $\mathsf{C u}_{2}\mathsf{S}$ particles were successfully prepared.
## 2.6. Characterizations
X-ray diffractometer (XRD) (D8 ADVANCE) equipped with Cu $\mathsf{K}\mathsf{\ensuremath{\upalpha}}$ radiation was utilized to analyze the crystal structures of dried sample powders. The morphology and microstructure characterizations were performed on a field-emission scanning electron microscope (FESEM, ZEISS, Ultra-55) at $5.0~\mathrm{kV}$ and transmission electron microscope (TEM, JEOL-2100) at $120\mathrm{kV}.$ The element distribution was investigated using energy dispersive $\mathsf{X}$ -ray spectrometry (EDS) elemental analysis attached on JEOL-2100. In order to identify the element states of products, X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) measurements were conducted on a Kratos XSAM-800 with a radiation source of Al $\mathsf{K}\mathsf{\mathfrak{A}}$ X-ray. Witec Alpha 300 confocal microscope Raman spectrometer was applied to examine the carbon structure. The porosity was analyzed through ${\sf N}_{2}$ sorption measurements at $77~\mathrm{K}$ using an Autosorb IQ Gas Sorption instrument. BET surface area, the mesopores with width over $2\ \mathrm{nm}$ were analyzed by Barrett-Joyner-Halenda (BJH) method and the micropores were studied by Horvath-Kawazoe (H-K) method. The mass contents of metal sulfides and carbon in the products were determined through Thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) using a METTLER TOLEDO TGA2. The thickness of product was determined by using a Bruker Dimension Icon atomic force microscope (AFM).
## 2.7. Electrochemical measurements
Active material, carbon nanotubes (CNTs) and polyvinylidene fluoride (PVDF) were mixed in N-methyl pyrrolidone (NMP) solution with a weight ratio of 7:2:1 to produce a homogeneous slurry. The as-prepared slurry was then spread over the copper foil evenly, followed by a drying process in a vacuum oven at $70^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ . Coin half cells were assembled in a glove-box $<0.1$ ppm, $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and $0_{2}$ ) filled with Ar with the cut electrode slice as the working electrode, Li metal as the counter electrode and Celgard 2400 membrane as the separator. The electrolyte was $1\mathrm{MLiPF_{6}}$ in a mixture of ethylene carbonate (EC) and dimethyl carbonate (DMC) (1:1 in volume). To evaluate the electrochemical performance of the above-assembled cells, galvanostatic cycling was carried out over the $0.01{-}3.0\mathrm{~V~}$ voltage range on a battery test system (LandCT2001A) at room temperature. The cyclic voltammetry (CV) tests ranging from 0.01 to $3.00\mathrm{V}$ and electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) in a fixed frequency range of $0.1~\mathrm{MHz}$ to $0.01~\mathrm{Hz}$ were conducted on an electrochemical measurement system (AutoLab-PGSTAT302N).
## 3. Results and discussion
The synthesis process of ultrathin 2D ${\mathsf{C o-C u}}_{2}{\mathsf{S@C}}$ nanosheets is schematically shown in Fig. 1. Firstly, $\mathtt{C u}_{2}0$ nanocubes are prepared via a co-precipitation reaction of $\mathsf{C u C l}_{2}$ and $\mathsf{N a O H}$ followed by a reduction of sodium ascorbate. After that, with a further bottom-up method by using $\mathtt{C u}_{2}0$ nanocubes as templates at room temperature, 2D Co-doped $\mathsf{C u}(\mathsf{H B T C}){\mathsf{-1}}$ nanosheets are formed. Note that both small molecular (i.e., water and oxygen) and proper cobalt anions are needed to react with $\mathtt{C u}_{2}0$ in this process. Finally, the ultrathin 2D ${\mathsf{C o-C u}}_{2}{\mathsf{S@C}}$ hybrid composites can be obtained by a sulfurization of 2D ${\mathsf{C o-C u}}({\mathsf{H B T C}}){\mathsf{-1}}$ at $600~^{\circ}\mathsf C$ under flowing Ar gas, during which the sulfur powders are sublimated and react with metal species to form ${\mathsf{C o-C u}}_{2}S$ while the organic linker $(\mathsf{H}_{3}\mathsf{B T C})$ is transformed to carbon, resulting in the in-situ formation of strongly-coupled heterostructure composed of embedding Co$\mathsf{C u}_{2}\mathsf{S}$ within 2D ultrathin carbon nanosheet.
The morphologies and microstructures of the as-prepared products were checked by FESEM and TEM, respectively. As shown in Fig. S1, the low-magnification FESEM images clearly present that the obtained 2D $\mathsf{C o-C u(H B T C)}\mathrm{-}1\mathrm{-}1$ precursors are rectangle nanosheets with size of $2\mathrm{-}3\ \upmu\mathrm{m}$ . As expected, the 2D structure could be kept well after sulfurization treatment (Fig. 2a and b). Interestingly, the ${\mathsf{C o-C u_{2}S@C}}$ composite shows an ultrathin nanosheet-like structure after sulfurization. Such a decrease of the thickness for the 2D nanosheet could be ascribed to the architecture shrunk caused by the thermal stress and decomposition. Moreover, a higher-magnification FESEM image (Fig. 2c) also reveals that the smooth surfaces for precursors have been changed to the rough and porous surfaces for 2D ${\mathsf{C o-C u}}_{2}{\mathsf{S@C}}$ composites. In consistent with the FESEM observations, the ultrathin 2D sheetlike structure can be clearly seen in TEM image (Fig. 2d). As shown in an enlarged TEM image (Fig. 2e), plentiful black dots with an average diameter of $12\pm4.5~\mathrm{{nm}}$ are uniformly distributed on the nanosheets, suggesting the well-coupling between particles and carbon sheet. Two districts are perceived from the highresolution TEM image (Fig. 2f), where clear lattice fringes with a spacing of $0.324\ \mathrm{nm}$ indexed to the (1 0 2) crystal planes of ${\mathsf{C o}}{\mathsf{-C u}}_{2}S$ and the surrounding disordered area corresponded to amorphous carbon. Note that lattice defects can also be clearly identified (Fig. 2f and Fig. S2). EDS element mapping results in Fig. $2\mathrm{g-k}$ confirms the coexistence of S, Cu, C, and Co elements within the nanosheet. EDS spectra recorded from the middle sheet in Fig. S4 confirms that the atomic percentage of Co is ${\sim}0.43\%$ . Furthermore, the ultrathin feature is corroborated by an AFM image, which shows an average thickness of ${\sim}4.3~\mathrm{nm}$ for 2D ${\mathsf{C o-C u}}_{2}{\mathsf{S@C}}$ composite (Fig. 2l). For comparison, the morphologies of 3D $\mathsf{C u}_{2}S@{\mathsf{C}}$ and 2D $\mathsf{C u}_{2}S@{\mathsf{C}}$ are also investigated by FESEM and the results are presented in Fig. S3, from which 3D $\mathsf{C u}_{2}S@{\mathsf{C}}$ has the octahedral structures with rough surfaces while 2D $\mathtt{C u}_{2}S@C$ shows a similar morphology to that of 2D ${\mathsf{C o-C u_{2}S@C}}$ composite. Nevertheless, the AFM result (Fig. S5) suggests that 2D $\mathsf{C u}_{2}\mathsf{S@C}$ composite possesses a slightly thinner thickness of ${\sim}3.4~\mathrm{nm}$ than that of 2D ${\mathsf{C o-C u}}_{2}{\mathsf{S@C}}$ , implying that cobalt doping might cause the increase of thickness.
The phase structures of the as-synthesized hybrid composites were characterized by XRD. Fig. S6a shows the XRD patterns of precursors, indicating the successful synthesis of HKUST-1 and $\mathtt{C u}$ (HBTC)-1. Notably, although the 2D Co-doped precursor has a semblable diffraction pattern with $\mathsf{C u}(\mathsf{H B T C}){\mathsf{-1}}$ , the further comparison between them (Fig. S6b) demonstrated that the positions of peaks in Co-Cu(HBTC)-1 are slightly shifted to a lower angle direction. As presented in Fig. 3a, the sulfurized products i.e. 2D $\mathsf{C u}_{2}\mathsf{S@C}$ and 3D $\mathsf{C u}_{2}\mathsf{S@C}$ show similar XRD patterns, where the whole diffraction peaks can be assigned to chalcocite $\mathsf{C u}_{2}\mathsf{S}.$ -type phase (JCPDS card No. 72-1071). A slight shift of peak positions to a lower angle direction caused by Co doping is observed in the XRD pattern of 2D Co$\mathsf{C u}_{2}S@{\mathsf{C}}$ when compared with $\mathsf{C u}_{2}S@C$ . Moreover, due to the fact that no diffraction peaks of Co or its sulfides are found in the XRD pattern of 2D ${\mathsf{C o-C u}}_{2}{\mathsf{S@C}}$ nanosheet, it could be concluded the successful doping of Co into $\mathtt{C u}_{2}S$ lattice. On the other hand, the broad peak between 20 and 35 degree is attributed to carbon.
To examine the chemical states of the elements on the surface of the sulfurized products, X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) investigations were performed. The C 1s spectrum in Fig. 4a describes two main peaks centered at 284.8 and $285.5\ \mathrm{eV}$ at the representative of ${\mathsf{C}}{\mathrm{-}}{\mathsf{C}}/{\mathsf{C}}{\mathrm{=}}{\mathsf{C}}$ bond and $c{-}s$ bond, respectively [21]. The existence of $c{-}s$ bond reveals the coupling between carbon and sulfur atoms. The oxidation state of $\mathtt{C o}\ 2\mathtt{p}$ is shown in Fig. 4b, where two pairs of spin-orbit doublets are displayed, i.e., Co $2\mathsf{p}_{3/2}$ located at 781.0 eV and Co $2{\tt p}_{1/2}$ located at $798.8\ \mathrm{eV},$ demonstrating the presence of Co species [21]. The highresolution $\mathtt{C u2p}$ spectrum of 2D ${\mathsf{C o-C u}}_{2}{\mathsf{S@C}}$ is deconvoluted into two spin orbits at $952.5\ \mathrm{~eV}$ for $\mathtt{C u\ 2p_{1/2}}$ and $932.6\ \mathrm{~eV}$ for $\mathtt{C u}$ $2\mathsf{p}_{3/2}$ , with an energy difference of ${\sim}20~\mathsf{e V}$ [59]. Additionally, the four peaks centered at 162.8, 161.7, 165.2 and 164.1 eV are assigned to Cu-S $2{\tt p}_{1/2}$ , $\mathrm{Cu}{-}S2\mathsf{p}_{3/2}$ , $\mathsf{C}{\mathrm{-}}\mathsf{S}2\mathsf{p}_{1/2}$ and $\mathrm{C}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{S}2\mathrm{p}_{3/2}$ , respectively [59,60]. Notably, the four peaks for Cu $2{\tt p}_{1/2}$ , Cu $2\mathsf{p}_{3/2}$ , Cu-S $2{\tt p}_{1/2}$ and $\mathrm{Cu}{-}S2\mathrm{p}_{3/2}$ shift to lower binding energy upon introducing cobalt into the $\mathtt{C u}_{2}\mathtt{S}$ lattice when compared with those of bare 2D $\mathsf{C u}_{2}S@{\mathsf{C}}$ and 3D $\mathsf{C u}_{2}S@{\mathsf{C}}$ (Figs. S8a and S8b). These deviations could be due to a changed electronic structure caused by cobalt doping [61,62].
Copper sulfide is a kind of conversion-typed anode materials, which has reversible oxidation-reduction reaction with $\mathrm{Li}^{+}$ in the discharge and charge process [63]:
$$
\mathrm{Cu_{2}S+2L i^{+}+2e^{-}\leftrightarrow L i_{2}S+C u}
$$
The lithium storage mechanism and properties of the 2D Co$\mathtt{C u}_{2}S@C$ nanosheets are firstly studied by cyclic voltammetry (CV). Fig. 5a manifests the first CV curves at a sweep rate of $0.1\ \mathrm{mV}\ s^{-1}$ in the voltage range of $0.01{-}3.0\mathrm{V}$ vs $\mathrm{Li/Li^{+}}$ . In the first cathodic scan, a broad peak ranged $0.3\mathrm{-}0.9\:\mathrm{V}$ and two sharp peaks located at around 2.10, and $1.56{\mathrm{V}}$ are observed. The broad peak at $0.3\mathrm{-}0.9\:\mathrm{V}$ is related to the irreversible formation of solid electrolyte interphase (SEI) film. It is likely to associate the small peak at $2.10\ \mathrm{eV}$ with the adsorption of ${\mathrm{Li}}^{+}$ on the surface of composite [59]. The peak at $1.56\mathrm{~V~}$ is attributed to the reduction of ${\mathsf{C u}}^{+}$ and the formation of $\mathrm{Li}_{2}S$ , which slightly moves to a lower voltage in the subsequent cycles. In the first anodic scan, two peaks centered at $1.91\mathrm{\V}$ and $2.33{\mathrm{~V~}}$ are identified, which could be due to the oxidation of Cu and the formation of $\mathrm{Li}_{2}S$ [59,64]. In the subsequent three cathodic-anodic scans, the CV curves are almost overlapped, indicating the great reversibility of the electrochemical reaction in the 2D ${\mathsf{C o-C u_{2}S@C}}$ composite electrode. Fig. 5b displays the first, fifth and 100th discharge-charge voltage profiles of the 2D Co$\mathsf{C u}_{2}\mathsf{S@C}$ composite electrode at a current density of $0.5\mathrm{Ag}^{-1}$ . There are several plateaus consistent with the previous analysis from CV result in the profiles (Fig. 5a). Note that a high initial discharge capacitance of $1356\ \mathrm{mAh}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ and a reversible charge capacitance of $858\mathrm{~mAh~g^{-1}}$ are observed with an initial columbic efficiency of $63.3\%$ , mainly resulting from the formation of SEI film. The result of cycle performance at $0.5\mathrm{~A~g^{-1}}$ is presented in Fig. 5c. After 300 cycles, 2D Co- $\cdot\mathsf{C u}_{2}S@C$ nanosheet electrode can deliver a reversible discharge capacity of $780\ \mathrm{mAh}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , which is not only much higher than those of 2D $\mathsf{C u}_{2}S@{\mathsf{C}}$ $670\mathrm{mAh}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ ), 3D $\mathsf{C u}_{2}S@{\mathsf{C}}$ ( $234\mathrm{mAh}\:\mathrm{g}^{-1}.$ and pure $\mathtt{C u}_{2}S$ $(206\mathrm{\mAh\g^{-1}},$ , but also outperforms the recently reported Cu-based anode materials (Table S1). The rate capability of 2D ${\mathsf{C o-C u_{2}S@C}}$ nanosheet electrode is studied at different current densities ranging from 0.2 to $5\mathsf{A g}^{-1}$ (shown in Fig. 5d). At current densities of 0.2, 0.5, 1.0, 2.0 and $5.0\mathrm{~A~g^{-1}}$ , the electrode can deliver an average reversible capacity of 950, 783, 584, 386, and 209, respectively, which shows much higher capacities in comparison with those of 2D ${\mathsf{C u}}_{2}S@C$ , 3D $\mathsf{C u}_{2}\mathsf{S@C}$ and $\mathtt{C u}_{2}\mathtt{S}$ , especially at the high current density. When the current density returns to 0.2 ${\sf A}{\sf g}^{-1}$ , the electrode can still recover to a reversible capacity as high as 988 mAh $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ . To further investigate the stability of 2D Co$\mathsf{C u}_{2}\mathsf{S@C}$ nanosheet electrode, a long-term cycling performance is carried out at a current density of 1 A $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ after activation (Fig. 5e). It is obviously seen that after 1000 cycles, the electrode still exhibits a reversible capacity of $570\mathrm{~mAh~g^{-1}}$ , showing an excellent cycle stability. Moreover, after 300 cycles at $0.5\mathrm{~A~g^{-1}}$ , the batteries were disassembled and the morphologies of the 2D ${\mathsf{C o-C u}}_{2}{\mathsf{S@C}}$ and $\mathtt{C u}_{2}S$ electrodes were characterized by FESEM. As seen in Fig. S9, the pristine structure of $\mathtt{C u}_{2}S$ is dramatically damaged, while 2D structure in ${\mathsf{C o-C u}}_{2}{\mathsf{S@C}}$ is well-maintained as expected, demonstrating its good structural integrity. By hybridizing with carbon nanosheet, the volume variation of electrode is also greatly alleviated, and the structural stability is efficiently facilitated.
To investigate the electrochemical lithium storage kinetics of 2D ${\mathsf{C o-C u}}_{2}{\mathsf{S@C}}$ nanosheet electrode, CV tests at different scan rates ranging from 0.2 to $1.0\mathrm{\mV\}\mathsf{s}^{-1}$ were carried out after chargedischarge cycle testing. As shown in Fig. 6a, the current increases with the scan rate. The electrochemical lithium storage kinetics can be analyzed by the following equations [65,66]:
$$
\begin{array}{l}{i={\textsf{a}}\cdot{\boldsymbol{\nu}}^{\flat}}\\ {\log i={\mathsf{b}}\cdot\log\nu+\log{\mathsf{a}}}\end{array}
$$
where a and b mean variable constants, i is the current, and $\upsilon$ is the scan rate. Four peaks are picked to modulate the relationship between log i and log $\phantom{}\nu.$ By linear fitting, the values of b are 0.76 and 0.87 for peak 1 and peak 2 in the oxidation process, respectively (Fig. 6b). For peak 3 and 4 in the reduction process, the values of b are 0.85 and 0.84, respectively. The values of b represent that 2D ${\mathsf{C o-C u_{2}S@C}}$ nanosheet electrode includes diffusion-controlled and capacitive-controlled storage. A quantitative assessment to evaluate the contribution ratios of diffusion-controlled and capacitivecontrolled storage can be conducted based on the following equation:
$$
i_{(\mathrm{v})}=\mathbf{k}_{1}\cdot\boldsymbol{\nu}+\mathbf{k}_{2}\cdot\boldsymbol{\nu}^{1/2}
$$
where $i_{(\vee)}$ means the current at a certain voltage and $\mathbf{k}_{1}$ is a variable constant as well as $\mathbf{k}_{2}$ . By calculating $\mathbf{k}_{1}$ and $\mathbf{k}_{2}$ constants, the current contributed by diffusion and capacitive effect can be extracted. Fig. 6c demonstrates that capacitive effect totally contributes to
$83.6\%$ current at the scan rate of $0.4\mathrm{mV}s^{-1}$ . As shown in Fig. 6d, the contribution ratio of capacitive effect increases with the scan rate and well maintains over $80\%$ . At a sweep rate of $1\mathrm{mV}s^{-1}$ , it could even reach as high as $92.9\%$ . The lithium storage kinetics of 3D $\mathsf{C u}_{2}\mathsf{S@C}$ and 2D $\mathsf{C u}_{2}\mathsf{S@C}$ are further investigated. As displayed in Fig. S10 and Fig. S11, only $74.1\%$ and $88.3\%$ of total capacity are resulted from the capacitive contribution for 3D $\mathsf{C u}_{2}\mathsf{S@C}$ and 2D $\mathsf{C u}_{2}\mathsf{S}$ at $1\:\mathrm{\mV}\:\mathrm{\s^{-1}}$ , respectively, much smaller than that of 2D ${\mathsf{C o-C u_{2}S@C}}$
The dominant of capacity contribution from pseudocapacitive reaction for 2D ${\mathsf{C o-C u_{2}S@C}}$ could be ascribed to 2D structure and cobalt doping. Firstly, the ultrathin 2D structure of 2D ${\mathsf{C o-C u_{2}S@C}}$ and 2D $\mathsf{C u}_{2}S@{\mathsf{C}}$ plays an essential role in narrowing $\mathrm{Li}^{+}$ diffusion and increasing the contact surface with electrolyte, which could greatly boost the kinetics when compared with octahedral structure of 3D $\mathsf{C u}_{2}S@{\mathsf{C}}$ . Furthermore, cobalt doping can create a wealth of lattice defects as demonstrated in the above-presented highresolution TEM images (Fig. S2). These defects have been proven to have lower adsorption energy, resulting in energetically favorable structures to alleviate the ion diffusion during lithiation and delithiation process [34,56,67]. The electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) measurements are also carried out to further verify the fast lithium storage kinetics of 2D ${\mathsf{C o-C u_{2}S@C}}$ (Fig. S12). As presented in Fig. S13a, all of the electrochemical impedance spectra are fitted with separate semicircles in high and middle frequency as well as a straight line in low frequency, which are related to SEI film resistance $\left(R_{s}\right)$ , change transfer resistance $\left(R_{\mathrm{ct}}\right)$ , and Warburg impedance $(W_{0})$ , respectively. It is noticeable that 2D ${\mathsf{C o}}{\mathsf{-C u}}_{2}{\mathsf{S}}$ has the smallest semicircle among all measured composites, revealing the lowest charge transfer resistance (Fig. S13b). Additionally, in the low frequency, the lines of 2D ${\mathsf{C o-C u_{2}S@C}}$ and 2D $\mathsf{C u}_{2}S@{\mathsf{C}}$ composites are much straighter than that of 3D $\mathsf{C u}_{2}S@C$ , indicating the facilitated lithium ion diffusion. Moreover, the amount of doped Co may have an important influence on the thickness of composite. FESEM images shown in Fig. S14 suggest the thickness of composite increase with the increasing of added cobalt. The cycle performance (Fig. S15) and EIS results (Fig. S16) of composites with different cobalt contents demonstrate that the 2D Co- $\mathsf{C u}_{2}S@{\mathsf{C}}$ has the highest specific capacity and the smallest $R_{c t}$ in comparison with 2D ${\mathsf{C o-C u}}_{2}{\mathsf{S}}@{\mathsf{C}}{\mathsf{-}}2$ and 2D $\mathsf{C o-C u}_{2}\mathsf{S}@\mathsf{C}\mathsf{-}3$ . By cobalt doping, the electrochemical property of electrode can be improved. Nevertheless, with the continuous addition of cobalt, the thickness of composites increases, which may lead to a decline of conductivity and unsatisfied electrochemical performance. In short, 2D ${\mathsf{C o-C u_{2}S@C}}$ electrode renders an extraordinary electric conductivity and prominent lithium ion diffusion as a result of synergetic effects of deliberately-designed 2D structure and appropriate cobalt doping, which leads to a superior electrochemical property for LIB.
## 4. Conclusions
In summary, ultrathin 2D ${\mathsf{C o-C u}}_{2}{\mathsf{S@C}}$ nanosheets have been synthesized through a novel 2D MOF-driven strategy, which involves the preparation of well-defined 2D $\mathsf{C o-C u(H B T C)}_{-1}$ nanosheet precursors and subsequent sulfurization of the precursor at moderate temperature. The as-synthesized 2D ${\mathsf{C o-C u_{2}S@C}}$ nanosheets simultaneously integrates several appealing design rationales for high-performance electrodes including the porous ultrathin feature of architecture, remarkable conductivity and excellent mechanical stability of strongly coupled carbon nanosheets. As compared with those of 2D $\mathsf{C u}_{2}S@C$ and 3D $\mathsf{C u}_{2}S@{\mathsf{C}}$ , the electrochemical property of 2D ${\mathsf{I C o-C u_{2}S}}\oplus C$ composite was significantly improved by constructing 2D architecture and cobalt doping, representing a new record for copper sulfides-based anodes [59,60,64]. The current work may provide guidance for the structural design of advanced transition metal-based materials, which shows great potential to develop advanced materials for next-generation anodes of LIBs.
